<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecclesiastical History of the English People</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">731</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Historia ecclesiastica gentis Anglorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ecclesiastical History of the English People is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> history of the English church and peoples completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> in 731. Running from Caesar's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to Bede's own day, it describes the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdoms and is the principal source for their early history. It popularised dating by the Anno Domini era and was translated into Old English in the late ninth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecclesiastical-history-of-the-english-people.jpg" alt="Ecclesiastical History of the English People" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bede · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beda_Petersburgiensis_f3v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
